     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · TechCrunch · 2026-10-24 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>AI Agents Integrate into Text Messaging for Daily Tasks</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#AI</span> <span>#Technology</span> <span>#Messaging</span> <span>#Productivity</span></div>
       <p>A growing number of AI agents are now accessible via text messages, offering an alternative to downloading new applications. These agents can recall context, integrate with existing apps and services, and perform various tasks on a user's behalf, including scheduling appointments, organizing calendars, researching trips, sending emails, making reservations, online shopping, and setting reminders.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-24/ai-agents-integrate-into-text-messaging-for-daily-tasks.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Among the notable options, Instinct has garnered significant attention, achieving a $10 billion valuation after a $1 billion funding round in September 2026. Instinct functions as an always-available agent capable of taking actions, connecting to services like email and Google Workspace, and assisting with tasks from trip planning to subscription cancellations. In September, Instinct began assigning dedicated email addresses to user assistants. Ask Proxy, another agent, distinguishes itself by aiming to anticipate user needs, operating as a proactive chatbot for event planning, gift ideas, and daily decisions. It can process photos and offers a work-focused version, with personal use costing $1 per day and business pricing starting at $15 per employee per month.</p><p>Caddy, an AI assistant for iPhone (iMessage) and Android (RCS) users, transforms scattered phone information into actionable items without requiring a separate app. It connects to calendars and conversations to identify tasks, add events, set reminders, and conduct research. Caddy has been in public beta since April 2026. Comma is designed for comprehensive task management across work and personal life, operating across browsers, computers, and files, and accessible via messaging apps like Signal, Telegram, and WeChat. It completes tasks, checks its own work, and provides proactive notifications and daily briefings.</p><p>Fambot serves as an AI-powered &quot;chief of staff&quot; for families, consolidating elements of family life such as school communications, activities, and meal planning into an organized plan. Available on iOS, Android, web, and SMS, Fambot sends daily summaries of upcoming events and to-dos, connecting to services like Gmail and Google Calendar. It launched its beta in early September 2026, raising $3.5 million in pre-seed funding, and is currently free. Folk, an AI assistant accessible through iMessage, WhatsApp, and Telegram, manages reminders, researches topics, tracks flights, and handles emails, running on its own private cloud and capable of executing multi-step tasks. Its beta launched in May 2026, offering a free version and a Pro subscription for $8.33/month.</p><p>What to watch: The continued expansion of AI agent capabilities and their integration into daily digital routines.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft accurately synthesizes the details provided about various AI agents, correctly attributing their features, funding, and pricing models.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/24/all-the-ai-agents-that-can-live-in-your-text-messages/" rel="noopener">TechCrunch</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
