     <article class="story single" data-beat="entertainment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENTERTAINMENT</span> · Bollywood Hungama · 2026-10-24 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Bombay High Court Seeks CINTAA Explanation Over October 20 Elections</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#CINTAA</span> <span>#Bombay High Court</span> <span>#FWICE</span> <span>#Elections</span></div>
       <p>The Bombay High Court has sought an explanation from the Cine and Television Artistes Association (CINTAA) regarding its decision to hold elections and announce results on October 20, 2026. This development follows a contempt petition filed by the Federation of Western India Cine Employees (FWICE), which accuses CINTAA of disregarding earlier judicial orders, as reported by Times Now.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-24/bombay-high-court-seeks-cintaa-explanation-over-october-20-e.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>On October 22, Justice Aarti Sathe issued a show cause notice to CINTAA, stating that the material presented to the court indicated a prima facie case of wilful disobedience and breach of undertaking. Concurrently, the court has restrained the association from implementing the outcome of the elections held two days prior.</p><p>FWICE's petition alleges that CINTAA failed to comply with specific directions issued by the Bombay High Court on September 11 and October 19. These previous orders mandated that all parties involved in the dispute refrain from taking any further steps that could affect the court's directions while a Notice of Motion remained pending before the trial court. Despite these restrictions, CINTAA proceeded with its election process and declared the results.</p><p>The show cause notice, issued under Rule 9(1) of the Contempt of Courts (Bombay High Court Rules, 1994), requires CINTAA to explain why proceedings under the Contempt of Courts Act, 1971, should not be initiated against it for the alleged disregard of court orders and/or wilful breach of undertaking. FWICE was represented by Advocates Saveena Bedi Sachar and Ashok Shetty. The court has not yet made a final determination on the contempt petition, and the matter is scheduled to come up again before the Bombay High Court on November 18, 2026.</p><p>What to watch: The Bombay High Court will revisit the matter on November 18, 2026, to determine CINTAA's response to the contempt allegations.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is a precise summary of the provided text, correctly identifying the legal status of the contempt proceedings and the court's specific actions.</p>
