     <article class="story single" data-beat="finance">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">FINANCE</span> · Livemint Money · 2026-10-24 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Central Government Employees Await July 2026 DA Hike Announcement</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#DA hike</span> <span>#Dearness Allowance</span> <span>#Dearness Relief</span> <span>#central government</span></div>
       <p>Central government employees and pensioners are currently awaiting the announcement of the July 2026 dearness allowance (DA) and dearness relief (DR) revision. Attention has shifted to the next weekly Union Cabinet meeting, which could take place on October 28, as a possible occasion for the announcement, though the final rate and timing remain subject to official confirmation.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-24/central-government-employees-await-july-2026-da-hike-announc.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The Centre typically announces major policy decisions following its weekly Cabinet meetings. However, the Department of Expenditure under the Finance Ministry can also issue a notification approving a revised DA rate without a Cabinet announcement. Employees had previously anticipated an announcement after a Cabinet meeting expected on October 21, following Dussehra, but this meeting was not held. Earlier, on October 16, the government announced a non-performance-linked bonus for central government employees but made no mention of the DA revision.</p><p>According to an Economic Times report, the July 2026 DA revision is expected to bring an increase of approximately three percentage points, raising the existing DA rate from 60% to 63%. This calculation is based on the average retail inflation recorded between July 2025 and June 2026, applied to the prescribed DA calculation formula. For ordinary savers and taxpayers who are central government employees or pensioners, this means a direct increase in their monthly income.</p><p>Dearness allowance is calculated as a percentage of an employee's basic pay, so any increase in the DA rate directly boosts the DA component of their salary, resulting in a higher gross payout. The transport allowance (TPTA), another salary component, is also linked to DA and will increase following this revision, adding to the overall financial benefit. The government usually reviews and revises DA and DR twice annually, with revised rates taking effect from January 1 and July 1. While the January revision is generally announced in March or April, the July adjustment typically comes in October. If the announcement is made after the effective date, eligible employees and pensioners receive arrears for the intervening period.</p><p>What to watch: The outcome of the October 28 Cabinet meeting for an official announcement.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Effectively synthesizes the context of the DA hike, the specific expectations, and the reasons for the current delay.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.livemint.com/money/personal-finance/da-hike-news-july-2026-will-centre-announce-da-hike-on-oct-28-how-much-increase-can-employees-expect-check-details-11792841258779.html" rel="noopener">Livemint Money</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
