     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · The Verge · 2026-10-24 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Feds Propose Cyan Lights for Robotaxis to Signal Autonomous Operation</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Tech</span> <span>#Autonomous Vehicles</span> <span>#Robotaxis</span> <span>#NHTSA</span></div>
       <p>The National Highway Traffic Safety Administration (NHTSA) has proposed a standardized solution for driverless cars to indicate autonomous operation: cyan-colored lights. After years of debate involving flashing text, audio alerts, and custom lights, the federal agency released proposed changes to Federal Motor Vehicle Safety Standard (FMVSS) No. 108, specifically rewriting the rules to accommodate autonomous vehicles. This initiative aims to both cut red tape for human-free designs and standardize how robotaxis communicate with other road users.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-24/feds-propose-cyan-lights-for-robotaxis-to-signal-autonomous-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>NHTSA's proposal suggests cyan-colored marker lamps &quot;to clearly and consistently signal to law enforcement and other road users when an automated driving system is operating the vehicle.&quot; The choice of cyan is strategic, as red, amber, white, and flashing blue/red lights are already in use for existing vehicle functions like brakes, turn signals, headlights, and emergency vehicles, allowing cyan to fill a unique visual gap without confusion. Mercedes-Benz previously received state-level approval for turquoise lights on its Level 3 Drive Pilot cars, providing some precedent, though the automaker has since shifted to a Level 2++ system. NHTSA seeks to establish a national standard that major robotaxi operators such as Waymo, Nuro, and Zoox, along with automakers like GM and Mercedes, can uniformly adopt.</p><p>Under the proposed rule, the implementation of cyan marker lights would remain optional for manufacturers. However, by establishing official federal standards for their color, brightness, and location, NHTSA aims to ensure consistency and legality for any automaker choosing to incorporate them. Additionally, NHTSA is proposing to eliminate certain lighting requirements that are irrelevant for vehicles without a human driver, such as physical hazard switches, manual beam shifters, and turn levers. The agency is also updating the 12.8-volt testing standard to facilitate modern LED architectures and considering removing requirements dictating the positioning of high-beam lights relative to low-beam lights.</p><p>This regulatory update is part of a broader administrative push by the Department of Transportation to streamline the deployment of autonomous vehicles, making it easier for manufacturers to build cars without traditional human controls like steering wheels and pedals. Despite this push, regulators are still enforcing existing rules. Last month, NHTSA initiated an audit query into the Tesla Cybercab to examine the process and technical data used for its certification, specifically addressing the absence of features like sideview mirrors, pedals, and a steering wheel, which are currently mandated under FMVSS.</p><p>What to watch: The public comment period for these proposed rule changes.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft accurately synthesizes the technical details and regulatory context provided in the source text.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
