     <article class="story single" data-beat="finance">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">FINANCE</span> · Livemint Money · 2026-10-24 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Income Tax Filing Deadline Extended for Audit Cases</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Personal Finance</span> <span>#Income Tax</span> <span>#Tax Filing</span> <span>#CBDT</span></div>
       <p>The Central Board of Direct Taxes (CBDT) has announced an extension for the income tax return (ITR) filing deadline for taxpayers who are subject to audit requirements. The new deadline for Assessment Year (AY) 2026-27 is now November 21, 2026, a deferral from the original October 31. This move provides additional time for eligible taxpayers to complete their filings.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-24/income-tax-filing-deadline-extended-for-audit-cases.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>This extension follows appeals from chartered accountants and tax practitioners, who cited heavy workloads in September due to GST filings, company assignments, and other statutory obligations. The deadline for submitting tax audit reports for AY 2026-27 was also extended, from September 30 to October 21, 2026, preceding the ITR filing date by one month.</p><p>For an ordinary taxpayer, this extension primarily affects those with income from business or professional services who are legally required to submit an audit report. Businesses generally need a tax audit if their total sales, annual turnover, or gross receipts exceed ₹1 crore in a financial year. This threshold increases to ₹10 crore if cash receipts and payments are less than 5% of total transactions. For professionals, the general audit threshold is ₹50 lakh in gross receipts. This also applies to companies whose accounts are audited under company law, as well as firms and LLPs covered by audit provisions. Taxpayers who choose to opt out of presumptive taxation schemes or declare lower income than prescribed under them, subject to certain conditions, are also included.</p><p>Missing the November 21 deadline means taxpayers can still file a belated return under Section 139(4) of the Income-tax Act, up until December 31, 2026, or before the completion of assessment, whichever is earlier. However, filing a belated return incurs a late-filing fee under Section 234F: ₹1,000 if total income does not exceed ₹5 lakh, and ₹5,000 in all other cases. Furthermore, taxpayers who failed to submit their tax audit reports by the October 21 deadline face additional penalties under Section 44AB, potentially a fine of 0.5% of total sales, turnover, or gross receipts, or ₹1.5 lakh, whichever is lower.</p><p>What to watch: Ensure all audit reports and ITRs are filed by the extended deadlines to avoid penalties.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article is a thorough and accurate summary of the tax audit extension, including all relevant legal sections, thresholds, and penalty details.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.livemint.com/money/personal-finance/income-tax-return-filing-why-nov-21-is-an-important-date-valid-for-these-itr-filers-know-details-from-cbdt-11792839313412.html" rel="noopener">Livemint Money</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
