     <article class="story single" data-beat="health">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">HEALTH</span> · ET Healthworld · 2026-10-24 · editor 8/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Maharashtra Challenges Central Food Safety Regulations in High Court</h1>
       
       <p>Maharashtra has approached the High Court, challenging central food safety regulations that it claims usurp the powers of the state government. The petition was filed through the state commissioner of food safety, according to ET Healthworld.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-24/maharashtra-challenges-central-food-safety-regulations-in-hi.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The state argues that the original intent of the Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006, was to decentralise food regulation. This framework aimed to distribute regulatory authority rather than centralise it.</p><p>Historically, enforcement of the Act was carried out by the state commissioner of food safety and designated officers. The current central regulations are seen as a departure from this established decentralised model.</p><p>What to watch: The High Court's ruling on the petition and its implications for food regulation.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Accurate summary of the legal petition described. It captures the essence of the state's argument regarding decentralization.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://health.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/policy/maharashtra-moves-hc-against-central-food-safety-regulations-says-these-usurp-state-govts-powers/135159767" rel="noopener">ET Healthworld</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
