     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-24 · editor 9/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>NASA Accelerates Lunar Ambitions, Adopting Apollo's Urgency for Moon Base and Astronaut Landings</h1>
       
       <p>NASA is rapidly advancing its strategy to establish a moon base and return astronauts to the lunar surface within as little as two years, mirroring the urgency of the Apollo era. Administrator Jared Isaacman confirmed on Friday, October 24, 2026, that the space agency remains on schedule to launch Artemis III next summer. This mission will involve a critical docking exercise with lunar landers from Elon Musk's SpaceX and Jeff Bezos' Blue Origin, though it will be confined to Earth orbit and not include a moon trip for its four astronauts over a two-week period.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-24/nasa-accelerates-lunar-ambitions-adopting-apollos-urgency-fo.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The pivotal Artemis IV mission, which will mark NASA's first astronaut moon landing since the Apollo program, is still slated for 2028. Isaacman's update comes six months after the successful Artemis II flight, which saw four astronauts orbit the moon, representing humanity's first lunar journey in over half a century. NASA plans to maintain flexibility in its choice between SpaceX and Blue Origin for the Artemis IV lunar lander for as long as possible. Isaacman noted that the &quot;new space race,&quot; now against China rather than the Soviet Union, is &quot;much closer than it ever needed to be&quot; due to past delays, prompting the agency's accelerated pace by cutting bureaucratic inertia and boosting its workforce.</p><p>The renewed approach draws directly from Apollo's intense period between 1968 and 1972, which saw 24 astronauts travel to the moon and 12 land. Isaacman emphasized at a Kennedy Space Center news conference that this isn't &quot;tech industry pixie dust&quot; but &quot;our own history,&quot; a &quot;playbook this agency knows really well&quot; that propelled the journey from Apollo 7 to Apollo 11 in just nine months. Next summer, both the Blue Origin Blue Ghost and SpaceX Starship landers will launch for docking practice by the Artemis III crew, comprising three NASA astronauts and one Italian, aboard NASA's Orion capsule. This mission will necessitate three rapid launches from Cape Canaveral, with Orion docking first with Blue Ghost 270 miles (435 kilometers) above Earth, then with Starship in a slightly lower orbit, a sequence Associate Administrator Amit Kshatriya called &quot;pretty awesome.&quot;</p><p>NASA's planned moon base near the moon's south pole, a departure from Apollo's more equatorial sites, is set to see its first launch by year's end. A Pittsburgh company, whose initial lunar landing mission failed in 2024, will attempt a second mission, targeting the south polar region with a rover and other experiments. Officials state that numerous subsequent landers, rovers, and orbiters will follow, ensuring a moon car is already awaiting the first Artemis moonwalkers upon their arrival. To enhance speed and reduce risk for initial surface missions, astronauts will utilize a lighter, less complex spacesuit than the one Axiom Space is developing for longer, later excursions, with Axiom testing these &quot;sortie suits&quot; on the International Space Station next year. NASA astronaut Frank Rubio, part of the Artemis III crew, highlighted the ambitious nature of establishing a long-term presence, telling The Associated Press, &quot;One thing I've always believed and learned is don't bet against America.&quot;</p><p>What to watch: Progress on Artemis III docking tests and the initial robotic missions to the lunar south pole.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Excellent work integrating the full text details; accurate and thorough reporting on the Artemis program status and NASA's strategic shifts.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-nasa-apollo-urgency-moon-base.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
