     <article class="story single" data-beat="education">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">EDUCATION</span> · Education (aggregate) · 2026-10-24 · editor 8/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Study Abroad Revolution: 70% Target Budgets Below Rs 30 Lakh</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Education</span> <span>#Study Abroad</span></div>
       <p>A notable transformation is taking place in the realm of international education, characterized as the &quot;Rs 30 lakh study-abroad revolution.&quot; A report by India Today highlights that a significant majority, specifically 70% of students planning to pursue studies overseas, are now aiming for budgets that fall below Rs 30 lakh.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-24/study-abroad-revolution-70-target-budgets-below-rs-30-lakh.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>This emerging trend underscores a pronounced shift towards more economically viable international study options among Indian students. The emphasis on managing costs suggests a deliberate move to find quality global academic opportunities within a more accessible financial scope.</p><p>The data indicates an evolving strategy among students, who are actively seeking to balance the aspiration of international education with prudent financial planning. This development is poised to influence how educational institutions and advisors tailor their offerings and guidance for the Indian student demographic.</p><p>What to watch: The long-term impact on study abroad program development and student choices.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is a concise and accurate summary of the provided source information without unnecessary padding.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://news.google.?oc=5&amp;hl=en-US&amp;gl=US&amp;ceid=US:en" rel="noopener">Education (aggregate)</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
