     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-10-24 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Telangana to Unveil Aerospace and Defence Policy 2.0, Aims for Industrial Hub Status</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Telangana</span> <span>#Aerospace</span> <span>#Defence</span> <span>#Policy 2.0</span></div>
       <p>Telangana Chief Minister A. Revanth Reddy announced that the state will soon unveil its Aerospace and Defence Policy 2.0, aligning with the Telangana Rising Vision 2047. Speaking at the curtain-raiser for Raksha Expo-2026, Reddy declared Telangana's ambition to become the country's largest industrial hub for the defence industry, urging domestic and global companies to increase investments in defence manufacturing, aerospace, space technology, cybersecurity, artificial intelligence (AI), and drone technology.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-24/telangana-to-unveil-aerospace-and-defence-policy-20-aims-for.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Reddy highlighted Telangana's robust ecosystem, skilled human resources, research institutions, and industrial capabilities as key strengths for a leading role in strengthening India’s defence manufacturing sector. He emphasized Hyderabad's strategic importance within India’s defence ecosystem, citing its numerous research institutions and strategic establishments associated with the Indian Army and Indian Air Force. Despite being a landlocked state, Telangana contributes significantly to the Indian Navy, evidenced by the very low frequency (VLF) naval radar facility under construction in the Damagundam Reserve Forest in Vikarabad district.</p><p>The Chief Minister stressed that India must transition from importing defence technologies to exporting advanced ones, asserting that strong defence capabilities are crucial for preserving peace. He stated that Telangana has been working towards this vision and called for greater participation from industry leaders and investors to meet ambitious targets. Reddy encouraged companies to double their investment plans in the state, assuring them of government cooperation. He explicitly offered, &quot;Tell me what support you need from me to achieve this objective. I am ready to extend all necessary assistance,&quot; underscoring the government's commitment to fostering a conducive investment environment.</p><p>Reddy dismissed any notion of &quot;policy paralysis&quot; in Telangana, affirming that political and ideological differences would not impede investment and development. He articulated the government's clear approach and strong determination to advance economic growth, urging industry leaders to think big across strategic sectors. While Telangana currently accounts for over 2.5% of India’s population and contributes about 5% of the country’s GDP, Reddy aims to elevate its share in the national economy to 10% by 2047, with defence manufacturing, aerospace, and allied industries playing a pivotal role.</p><p>Investors are also invited to the second edition of the Invest Telangana Global Summit, scheduled to be held at Bharat Future City on December 7, 8, and 9.</p><p>What to watch: The details and implementation of Telangana's new Aerospace and Defence Policy 2.0.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The report captures the Chief Minister's vision and policy announcements accurately, maintaining the tone and detail of the full source text.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/telangana/telangana-to-unveil-new-aerospace-and-defence-policy-20-soon-says-revanth/article71621205.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
